WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.950
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Hi,
I'm Priyanka Vergadia.

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.290
And this is AI
Simplified, where we

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.860
learn to make our data useful.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.790
We're helping an
imaginary company

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.700
that is in the process of
building their machine learning

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:12.660
workflow.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.700
And to accelerate
AI innovation, they

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.160
are looking at Vertex
AI, which provides

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.580
tools for every step of the
machine learning workflow,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.700
from managing data sets, to
different ways of training

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.980
the model,
evaluating, deploying,

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.510
and making predictions.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.580
In the previous
episode, we started

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.380
by looking at the first part of
any ML project, the data sets.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.940
Vertex AI supports managed data
sets for image, tabular, text,

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.440
and video data.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.920
In this episode, let's move
further in the ML workflow

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.070
and talk about building
and training the models.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.220
The managed data sets we
created earlier feed directly

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.130
into the model
building and training.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.410
Although you can bring
external data sets

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.990
while building the
model, managed data sets

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.240
bring some added
benefits as I laid out

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:01.930
in the previous episode.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.990
We have two options
to train the model.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:08.250
We can use AutoML which allows
us to create high quality

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.310
custom models with minimal
effort and machine learning

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:12.180
expertise.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.930
And the second option is to
write custom model training

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.330
code, where you can
run custom training

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:21.900
applications in the cloud
using pre-built containers

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.170
of your own.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.490
Now when should you write
your own model code and then

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.170
should you choose AutoML?

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.100
It depends.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.180
So let's see.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.870
If your use case fits a
supported AutoML offering,

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:36.960
then starting with
AutoML is a great choice.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.220
And these use cases include
cases where you have images,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.440
or video classification,
or object

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.370
detection, text classification,
entity extraction

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:50.580
from the text, sentiment
analysis of text,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.310
tabular regression,
and classification.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.640
But if it does not fit
any of those categories,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.390
or your model takes some
mixed input type, such

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.610
as you have images
and then the metadata

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.460
is in the form of text,
then it makes sense

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.330
to use custom model.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.449
The other technical
factor could be

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:12.780
that you have specific
requirements for the underlying

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.540
model if you need
control over your model's

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.800
architecture, framework, or
the exported model assets.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.990
For example, maybe
your model needs

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.990
to be built in
TensorFlow or PyTorch.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.120
Then you use custom model.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.650
Otherwise, for the basic
use cases, just use AutoML.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.140
The next factor could
be how experienced

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.420
is your team with ML and AI.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.320
If you have a team with limited
machine learning expertise

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.950
in building custom models,
then exploring AutoML

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:48.510
makes sense before looking
into the custom model code

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.660
because it is the
lowest barrier to entry.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.500
Another deciding factor
could be your team size.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.730
Now if you have a small data
science team and less machine

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.550
learning expertise in
that team, it perhaps

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:06.660
makes more sense to work with
AutoML because custom model

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.800
code would require more
time and maintenance.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.420
And use AutoML if you want to
develop a quick initial model

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.840
to be used as a baseline
or a proof of concept,

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.540
which could eventually end up
being your production model.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:25.590
But if you are looking to
improve on your existing

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.720
baseline, or
heuristics, then you

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.660
might want to write
your own custom model

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.030
to test out the possibilities.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.430
Hopefully, these
factors have clarified

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:39.030
which path to take when it
comes to building the models.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.750
Now let's see how to build
and train the AutoML model

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.490
using Vertex AI.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.640
With that, onto the
Google Cloud console.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.700
We learned how to create data
sets in the previous episode.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.270
But for the sake
of completeness,

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:53.790
let's create a data set.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.550
In this example, we're
using credit card fraud

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.400
detection data set
available on BigQuery

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.490
public data sets to
create a credit card fraud

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:03.990
detection model.

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:06.870
I have included the link to
this data set below if you

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.700
would like to follow along.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.090
Once the data is uploaded,
we can analyze our data

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.310
in the rows and columns.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.510
Right from the data
set details page,

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:21.220
we can select Train New
Model, define our objective,

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.900
which is classification here
since we are labeling examples

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.200
as either fraudulent
or non fraudulent.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.150
This is where you
decide whether you

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:33.670
want to use AutoML
or custom training.

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.270
In this video, we're
focusing on AutoML.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.040
So we will select that.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.470
Stay tuned for the
next episode, where

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.910
I will cover a custom model.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.210
Next, we give our model a name.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.090
Under the Target
column, we select Class.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.970
This is an integer
indicating whether or not

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.930
a particular transaction was
fraudulent, zero for non fraud,

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:56.730
and one for fraud.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:01.100
In the advanced options, you can
specify a specific train test

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:05.060
split if you don't want Vertex
AI to handle that automatically

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:05.870
for you.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.940
In this case, we're using
the random assignment.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.480
But you can also
specify this manually

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.120
when you upload a
CSV of your data.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:17.630
Or you can have the data
split chronologically

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:19.790
if it has a time column.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:22.350
In the Choose Training
Options section,

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.380
we will go to the
Advanced option.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:28.310
And since this data is heavily
imbalanced, less than 1%

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:31.220
of the data here contains
fraudulent transactions,

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:34.650
we will choose the
AUC PRC option,

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.940
which will maximize
precision recall for the less

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:39.530
common class.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:43.310
In this case, we care more
about ensuring our model

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:47.490
can classify fraudulent
transactions correctly.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:50.360
Now the last step is
compute and pricing.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:54.230
Here we enter one as the number
of compute hours for our budget

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.530
and leave early
stopping enabled.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:00.740
Training your AutoML
model for one compute hour

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:03.620
is typically a good start for
understanding whether there

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:07.100
is a relationship between the
features and labels you've

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.240
selected.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.150
From there, you can
modify your features

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:14.870
and train for more time to
improve model performance.

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:16.850
Then we are ready
to start training.

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:20.330
From here, we can leave the
UI and will get an email when

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:22.670
our training job completes.

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:23.240
All right.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:24.800
So our model is ready.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.650
Let's explore the model
evaluation metrics.

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.110
We head to our Model
tab and evaluate

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:31.820
the model we just trained.

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:34.220
There are many
evaluation metrics here.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:37.730
We will focus on two, the
confusion matrix, and feature

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:38.900
importance.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:42.380
A confusion matrix tells us
the percentage of examples

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:46.190
from each class in our test
set that our model predicted

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:47.210
correctly.

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:48.890
In the case of an
imbalanced data

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:51.230
set like the one we're
dealing with here,

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:54.320
this is a better measure
of our model's performance

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.480
than overall accuracy.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:00.380
Remember that less than 1% of
the examples in our data set

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:02.040
were fraudulent transactions.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:06.500
So if our model accuracy is
99%, there is a good chance

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:11.150
that it's just randomly guessing
the non fraudulent class 99%

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:11.930
of the time.

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:16.010
That's why looking at our
model's accuracy for each class

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.320
is a better metric here.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.830
The confusion matrix shows
that our initial model

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:26.060
is able to classify 85%
of the fraudulent examples

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:28.130
in our test set correctly.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:30.560
This is pretty good,
especially considering

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.590
our significant
data set imbalance.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:37.790
Next, we could try training our
model for more compute hours,

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:42.350
or gathering more training data
to see if we can improve that

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:45.410
from this 85%.

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:47.570
Now this shows us
the features that

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:50.390
provided the biggest
signal to our model

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:51.860
when making predictions.

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:54.110
The feature
importance is one type

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:58.370
of explainable AI, a field
that includes various methods

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:02.030
for getting more insight into
how animal model is making

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:02.960
predictions.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:06.950
The feature importance chart
is calculated as an aggregate

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:12.020
by looking at all of our model's
predictions on our test set.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:14.840
It shows us the most
important features

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:16.950
across a batch of examples.

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:18.680
This chart would
be more exciting

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:22.730
if most of the features in our
data set were not obscured.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:26.090
We might learn, for example,
that the type of transaction,

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:29.420
transfer, deposit, et
cetera can be the biggest

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:30.980
indicators of fraud.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:34.789
In a real world scenario,
these feature importance values

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:37.500
could be used to help
us improve our model

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:40.760
and to have more confidence
in its prediction.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:44.250
We might decide to remove
the least important features

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:46.970
next time we train a
model, or to combine

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:51.680
two of our more significant
features into a feature cross

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.070
to see if this improves
our model's performance.

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:57.920
We are looking at feature
importance across a batch here.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.140
But we can also get
feature importance

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:03.170
for individual
predictions in Vertex AI.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:07.200
And we'll see how to do that
once we've deployed our model.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.740
Now that we have trained
model, the next step

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:11.990
is to create an
endpoint in Vertex AI.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:15.500
A model resource in Vertex AI
can have multiple endpoints

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:16.950
associated with it.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:20.030
And you can split traffic
between endpoints.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:23.450
On our model page, there
is Deploy and Test tab.

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:26.180
There we click
Deploy To Endpoint.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:30.320
Give your endpoint a name
like fraud version one or two.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.200
And then leave the traffic
splitting settings as is.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:36.740
And then select a machine type
for your model deployment.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:40.490
We used an n1-highcpu-2
here but you

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:43.040
can choose whichever
machine type you would like,

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.260
including one with GPUs.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:47.540
Then select Done
and click Continue.

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:50.300
Leave the selected
location settings as is

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:52.010
and then click Deploy.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:54.590
Your endpoint will take
a few minutes to deploy.

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:57.500
When it is completed, you
will see a green checkmark

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:00.000
next to it just like
my version one here.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.220
Now we are ready
to get predictions

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:03.750
on our deployed model.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:06.810
There are a few options for
getting model predictions,

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:08.700
the UI and the API.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:10.770
Let's check out both one by one.

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:14.280
On your model page where
your endpoint is shown,

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:16.950
scroll down to the Test
Your Model section.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:19.980
Here, Vertex AI has
chosen random values

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:22.290
for each of our model's
features that we can

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.730
use to get a test prediction.

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:27.450
You're welcome to change
these values if you like

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.820
and then scroll down to
the bottom of the page

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:31.380
and select Predict.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:33.640
In the prediction
results section,

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:36.510
you should see your models
predicted percentage

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.340
for each class.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:42.460
A confidence score of 0.99
for class zero for example

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:47.580
means that our model thinks this
example is 99% non fraudulent.

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:51.270
The UI is a great way to make
sure the deployed endpoint is

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:52.650
working as expected.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:54.360
But chances are
that you will want

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:58.350
to get prediction dynamically
via a REST API call.

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:01.080
To show you how to get
model predictions here,

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:02.760
let's set up a notebook.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:05.220
For that, we navigate
to Notebooks option

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.220
and select TensorFlow
Without GPUs.

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:10.710
Once created, open
the notebook instance.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:14.340
And then we open Python 3
notebook form the launcher.

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:17.580
In the notebook, we run
the command in a cell

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:20.340
to install the Vertex AI SDK.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:24.600
And below that, we add and
run a new cell and the example

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:26.200
from our test set.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:29.280
Next, we need to create
the endpoint object

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:32.490
and then change the endpoint
ID and project number

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:34.920
to your specific endpoint.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:39.030
You can get the endpoint from
the URL and the project ID

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:41.310
from the project dashboard.

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:44.970
We use that to make a prediction
request to our endpoint.

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:49.860
And we see our prediction
around 0.99 for the zero class,

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.720
which means the
model thinks there

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:57.030
is a 99% chance this
transaction is non fraudulent.

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:59.790
With that, we've learned
about the two options

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:04.080
to train our models in Vertex
AI, AutoML and custom training,

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.060
and when to use which one.

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:09.540
Then we went into the console
and trained an ML model

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:10.770
from scratch.

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:13.110
In the next episode
of AI Simplified,

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:16.620
we will look at building
and training a custom model.

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:18.960
In the meantime, let's
continue our discussion

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:20.050
in the comments below.

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:21.930
And don't forget to
like and subscribe

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.830
to catch that next episode.

